<section xml:id="rate-functions">
    <title>Functions Defined by Their Rates</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            When we learned about derivatives, we learned that they define <term>rates of change</term>.
            As consequences of the Mean Value Theorem, we learned that if a derivative <m>f'(x)</m>
            stays positive on an entire interval <m>(a,b)</m>, then the function <m>f(x)</m> for which it is the rate
            must be <term>increasing</term> on the same interval <m>(a,b)</m>.
            An end point can be included in the interval if <m>f(x)</m> is continuous up to and including the end point.
            A similar argument works to say that a <em>negative</em> rate of change implies the function is <term>decreasing</term> on the corresponding interval.
            And if the rate is <em>zero</em> on an interval (not just an isolated point), this implies the funciton is <term>constant</term> on the corresponding interval.
        </p>
        <p>
            A second derivative describes the rate of change of the first derivative.
            That is, <m>f''(x)</m> determines the <em>slope</em> of the graph of the <m>f'(x)</m> function.
            A changing rate of change <m>f'(x)</m> corresponds to a curve in the function <m>f(x)</m>,
            so the signs of <m>f''(x)</m> are used to describe <term>concavity</term> of <m>f(x)</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection>
        <title>Describing Function Behavior</title>

        <p>
            We can describe the behavior of a quantity <m>Q</m> represented by a function <m>Q(t)</m> by understanding its rate of change <m>Q'(t) = R(t)</m>.
            <ul>
                <li><p><m>Q</m> is <term>increasing</term> when its rate of change is <m>Q' = R</m> is positive on an interval,</p></li>
                <li><p><m>Q</m> is <term>decreasing</term> when its rate of change <m>Q' = R</m> is negative on an interval.</p></li>
                <li><p><m>Q</m> is <term>constant</term> when its rate of change <m>Q' = R</m> is zero on an interval.</p></li>
            </ul>
            We describe concavity for <m>Q</m> analogously, but now based on whether the rate <m>R</m> is increasing or decreasing.
            <ul>
                <li><p>
                    <m>Q</m> is <term>concave up</term> on <m>I</m> if <m>R</m> is increasing on <m>I</m>.
                    This in turn occurs when <m>Q''=R'</m> (the slope of the graph of <m>R</m>) is positive.
                </p></li>
                <li><p>
                    <m>Q</m> is <term>concave down</term> on <m>I</m> if <m>R</m> is decreasing on <m>I</m>.
                    This in turn occurs when <m>Q''=R'</m> is negative.
                </p></li>
                <li><p>
                    <m>Q</m> is <term>linear</term> (straight) on <m>I</m> if <m>R</m> is constant on <m>I</m>.
                    This in turn occurs when <m>Q''=R'</m> is zero.
                </p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            Concavity is closely related to the concept of acceleration (by which we also include the idea of deceleration). A constant rate of change leads to a linear relation. On a graph, this is a straight line. Concavity refers to a rate of change that is itself changing. This is acceleration. In physics, acceleration is caused by a force, so we can think of concavity as the effect on an object in the presence of a force.
        </p>

        <p>
            Suppose that a quantity has a positive rate of change (increasing) and is also concave up (an increasing rate of change). This would be like a car moving forward (positive rate) with a rocket pushing it forward (positive acceleration). The result would be that the car continues to go faster (increasing rate), covering ever increasing distances per unit time. A graph of the position would be rising (increasing) and bending up (concave up).
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-prototype-incr-ccu">
            <caption><m>Q</m> is an increasing and concave up function of <m>x</m>. The rate is positive and increasing.</caption>
            <image width="50%">
                <latex-image>
                <!-- CDATA prevents certain LaTeX code from being interpreted as xml -->
                <![CDATA[\tikzset{%
                }
                \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.0cm,y=1.0cm]
                \draw[->] (0,0) -- (5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,4) node[above] {$Q$};
                \draw (1,1) .. controls (3,1) and (3.75,2) .. (4,3);
                \end{tikzpicture}]]>
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>Next, suppose that a quantity has a positive rate of change (increasing) but is concave down (a decreasing rate of change). This would be like a car moving forward (positive rate) but with a rocket in reverse (negative acceleration). The car would still be moving forward, but the rocket is slowing it down. A graph of position in this case would be rising (increasing) but bending down (concave down).</p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-prototype-incr-ccd">
            <caption><m>Q</m> is an increasing and concave down function of <m>x</m>. The rate is positive but decreasing.</caption>
            <image width="50%">
                <latex-image>
                <!-- CDATA prevents certain LaTeX code from being interpreted as xml -->
                <![CDATA[\tikzset{%
                }
                \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.0cm,y=1.0cm]
                \draw[->] (0,0) -- (5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,4) node[above] {$Q$};
                \draw (1,1) .. controls (1.75,2.5) and (2.75,2.8) .. (4,3);
                \end{tikzpicture}]]>
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>If the rocket continues to exert a negative force, there will be a moment when all of the forward momentum is gone and then the car begins to go backwards. Consequently, we learn that a quantity that is concave down can switch from increasing to decreasing. Graphically, this is exactly what a parabola that opens down does. However, if the rocket is gradually reduced, we might be able to slow the car down without ever changing direction.</p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-prototype-ccd-incr-decr">
            <caption><m>Q</m> is a concave down function of <m>x</m> that is increasing for <m>x \lt a</m> and decreasing for <m>x \gt a</m>.</caption>
            <image width="50%">
                <latex-image>
                <!-- CDATA prevents certain LaTeX code from being interpreted as xml -->
                <![CDATA[\tikzset{%
                }
                \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.0cm,y=1.0cm]
                \draw[->] (0,0) -- (5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,4) node[above] {$Q$};
                \draw (1,1) .. controls (1.5,2.5) and (2,3) .. (2.5,3)
                    .. controls (3,3) and (3.5,2.5) .. (4,1);
                \fill (2.5,3)  circle[radius=2pt];
                \draw[-] (2.5,-0.2) -- (2.5,0.2) node[pos=1,above] {$a$};
                \end{tikzpicture}]]>
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
            Similar behaviors might be described for negative rates of change. This would correspond to a car that is already going backwards. Being concave up (increasing rate of change) corresponds to a positive acceleration (rocket force), which in this case is opposite the motion and would serve to slow the car down maybe to the point of reversing direction. Graphically, this corresponds to a dropping graph that is bending up (moving toward flat). Being concave down (decreasing rate of change) corresponds to negative acceleration (rocket force) which now is the same direction as the motion. This would cause the car to speed up (in the negative direction). Graphically, being concave down corresponds to a graph that is bending down and growing ever steeper.
        </p>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>Suppose <m>V</m> measures the volume of water (liters) in a container and that <m>V</m> is a function of time <m>t</m> (minutes) such that the rate of change (liters per minute) is also a function of time defined by <md>\frac{dV}{dt} = R(t) = 10-0.25t.</md> Describe the behavior of <m>V</m> and sketch a representative graph.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The rate of change of the volume in the container, <m>R(t)</m> determines the behavior of the volume. Because <m>R</m> has a negative slope <m>m=-0.25</m>, the rate is decreasing. This tells us that the volume is a concave down function. Solving the inequalities <m>R(t) \gt 0</m> and <m>R(t) \lt 0</m> will allow us to see when the rate is positive or negative, which will imply when the volume is increasing or decreasing, respectively.</p>
                <p>The inequalities are solved by solving the equation <m>R(t)=0</m> and then testing the inequalities in the resulting intervals. <md>
                    <mrow>10-0.25t = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>-0.25t = -10</mrow>
                    <mrow> t = \frac{-10}{-0.25} = 40</mrow>
                </md> Testing the sign of <m>R(t)</m> when <m>t \lt 40</m>, we find, for example <m>R(20) = 10-0.25(20) = 5</m>, that the rate is positive. Consequently, <m>V</m> is increasing when <m>t \lt 40</m>. On the other hand, testing the sign of <m>R(t)</m> when <m>t \gt 40</m>, such as <m>R(60) = 0-0.25(60)=-5</m>, we find that the rate is negative so that the volume is decreasing when <m>t \gt 40</m>.</p>
                <p>The graph of <m>R(t)</m>, shown below, is consistent with these analyses. The graph is decreasing (corresponding to the negative slope), above the axis for <m>t \lt 40</m> and below the axis for <m>t \gt 40</m>.</p>
                <figure>
                    <caption>A graph of the rate of change of volume of water as a function of time.</caption>
                    <image>
                        <latex-image>
                        <!-- CDATA prevents certain LaTeX code from being interpreted as xml -->
                        <![CDATA[\tikzset{%
                        }
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}
                        [ xmin=-5, xmax=75,
                            xlabel={$t$ [min]}, ylabel={$\displaystyle R(t)=\frac{dV}{dt}$ [L/min]},
                        ]
                        \addplot[dashed] coordinates {
                            (-5,0) (75,0)};
                        \addplot[thick, blue, domain=-5:75] (\x, 10-0.25*\x);
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    The graph of the volume therefore needs to be concave down, increasing for <m>t \lt 40</m> and decreasing for <m>t \gt 40</m>. We do not know the starting volume (it wasn't given), so the vertical positions
                    of the graph do not presently have specific meaning.
                    The initial value of 0 was chosen to represent whatever the starting volume happened to be.
                    The graph of this functions is shown below.
            </p>
                <figure>
                    <caption>A graph of the volume of water as a function of time relative to the starting height.</caption>
                    <image>
                        <latex-image>
                        <!-- CDATA prevents certain LaTeX code from being interpreted as xml -->
                        <![CDATA[\tikzset{%
                        }
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                        \begin{axis}
                        [ xmin=-5, xmax=75,
                            xlabel={$t$ [min]}, ylabel={$V(t)$ [L]},
                        ]
                        \addplot[dashed] coordinates {
                            (-5,0) (75,0)};
                        \addplot[thick, blue, domain=-5:75] (\x, 10*\x-0.125*\x^2);
                        \end{axis}
                        \end{tikzpicture}]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec_derivs_shape">
        <title>Complete Shape Analysis</title>
        <p>
            When we use both the first and second derivatives, we can find intervals for both monotonicity and concavity. This allows us to describe the shape of the graph.
        </p>
        <example xml:id="ex_derivs_to_shape">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Describe the behavior of <m>f(x)=x^4-12x^2+16x</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    We use <m>f'(x)</m> to understand where <m>f(x)</m> is increasing or decreasing, and <m>f''(x)</m> to understand where <m>f(x)</m> is concave up or concave down.
                    We start by calculating the derivatives so that we can do sign analysis.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(x) &amp;= x^4-12x^2+16x</mrow>
                        <mrow>f'(x) &amp;= 4x^3-24x+16</mrow>
                        <mrow>f''(x) &amp;= 12x^2-24</mrow>
                    </md>
                    To do sign analysis, we need to find the zeros of the functions. If the functions factor, that is the easiest way to accomplish that.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(x) &amp;= x(x+4)(x-2)^2</mrow>
                        <mrow>f'(x) &amp;= 4(x-2)(x^2+2x-2)</mrow>
                        <mrow>f''(x) &amp;= 12(x^2-2)</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    To do sign analysis of <m>f'(x)</m>, we find zeros as solutions of <m>4=0</m> (no solutions), <m>x-2=0</m> at <m>x=2</m>, and <m>x^2+2x-2=0</m> at <m>x=-1 \pm \sqrt{3}</m>, approximated by <m>x \approx -2.732</m> and <m>x \approx 0.732</m>.
                    Putting these on a number line, we can find the intervals we need to test: <m>(-\infty,-1-\sqrt{3})</m>, <m>(-1-\sqrt{3},-1+\sqrt{3})</m>, <m>(-1+\sqrt{3},2)</m>, and <m>(2,\infty)</m>.
                    Evaluating <m>f'(x)=4(x-2)(x^2+2x-2)</m> at a point in each interval, we get the following sign analysis summary.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig_fcn_shape_firstD_signs">
                    <caption>Sign analysis summary for <m>\displaystyle f'(x)=4(x-2)(x^2+2x-2)</m>.</caption>
                    <image xml:id="fcn_shape_firstD_signs" width="75%">
                        <description>sign analysis summary</description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.25cm]
\draw [&lt;-&gt;, line width=1] (-4,0) -- (4,0);
\draw [line width=1] (-2.732,-0.1) -- (-2.732,0.1) node [pos=0, below] {$-1-\sqrt{3}$} node [pos=1, above] {0};
\draw [line width=1] (0.732,-0.1) -- (0.732,0.1) node [pos=0, below] {$-1+\sqrt{3}$} node [pos=1, above] {0};
\draw [line width=1] (2,-0.1) -- (2,0.1) node [pos=0, below] {$2$} node [pos=1, above] {0};

\node [below] at (-3,-0.1) {-3};
\node [above] at (-3,0.1) {$-$};
\node [below] at (0,-0.1) {0};
\node [above] at (0,0.1) {$+$};
\node [below] at (1,-0.1) {0};
\node [above] at (1,0.1) {$-$};
\node [below] at (3,-0.1) {3};
\node [above] at (3,0.1) {$+$};
\end{tikzpicture}       
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    From the first derivative, we know that <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing on the intervals <m>(-\infty,-1-\sqrt{3}]</m> and <m>[-1+\sqrt{3},2]</m> and is increasing on the intervals <m>[-1-\sqrt{3},-1+\sqrt{3}]</m> and <m>[2,\infty)</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The second derivative <m>f''(x) = 12(x^2-2)</m> provides information about concavity. The zeros are at <m>x=-\sqrt{2}</m> and <m>x=\sqrt{2}</m>. A sign analysis summary of <m>f''(x)</m> is given below.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig_fcn_shape_secondD_signs">
                    <caption>Sign analysis summary for <m>\displaystyle f''(x)=12(x^2-2)</m>.</caption>
                    <image xml:id="fcn_shape_secondD_signs" width="75%">
                        <description>sign analysis summary</description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.25cm]
\draw [&lt;-&gt;, line width=1] (-4,0) -- (4,0);
\draw [line width=1] (-1.414,-0.1) -- (-1.414,0.1) node [pos=0, below] {$-\sqrt{2}$} node [pos=1, above] {0};
\draw [line width=1] (1.414,-0.1) -- (1.414,0.1) node [pos=0, below] {$\sqrt{2}$} node [pos=1, above] {0};

\node [below] at (-3,-0.1) {-3};
\node [above] at (-3,0.1) {$+$};
\node [below] at (0,-0.1) {0};
\node [above] at (0,0.1) {$-$};
\node [below] at (3,-0.1) {3};
\node [above] at (3,0.1) {$+$};
\end{tikzpicture}       
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    The second derivative test for concavity allows us to conclude that <m>f(x)</m> is concave up on the intervals <m>(-\infty,-\sqrt{2}]</m> and <m>[\sqrt{2},\infty)</m> and is concave down on the interval <m>[-\sqrt{2},\sqrt{2}]</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    A graph of <m>f(x)</m> illustrates these behaviors. It has a local minimum at <m>x=-1-\sqrt{3}</m>, a local maximum at <m>x=-1+\sqrt{3}</m>, and another local minimum at <m>x=2</m>.
                    <m>f(x)</m> also has points of inflection at <m>x=\pm \sqrt{2}</m>. In the graph, the extremes are marked with circular points, while the inflection points are marked as squares.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig_fcn_shape_graph">
                    <caption>Graph of <m>y=f(x)=x^4-12x^2+16x</m>.</caption>
                    <image xml:id="fcn_shape_graph" width="60%">
                        <description>sign analysis summary</description>
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.25cm]
                            \begin{axis}
                            [xmin=-5, xmax=4, ymin=-100, ymax=50, grid=both, axis lines=center, xlabel=$x$]
                            \addplot [domain=-5.2:5.2, samples=200, thick, blue] {x^4-12*x^2+16*x};
                            \addplot [samples at={-2.732,0.732,2}, only marks, blue] {x^4-12*x^2+16*x};
                            \addplot [samples at={-1.414,1.414}, only marks, mark=square*, solid, blue, fill=blue] {x^4-12*x^2+16*x};
                            \end{axis}
                            \end{tikzpicture}       
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>
        <p>
            While these examples focused on finding intervals where the function had the properties of increasing, decreasing, concave up and concave down, this information could also be used to create sketches of the function's behavior. This will be investigated further in the next section where we consider the idea of an antiderivative.
        </p>
    </subsection>    
</section>
